## Building a part

The artifact is a PROGRAM, not a mesh. Anything you can only produce by
hand-placing vertices is a thing you cannot re-run, re-parameterise or
hand back changed.

- One class per part. A part owns the objects it creates and exposes
  named build steps. A flat top-to-bottom script cannot be diagnosed
  when step four is wrong.
- Assemblies compose sub-builders and validate at the same granularity
  they build at. Validate the leg before you join six of them, or the
  failure you read is the assembly's and the bug is the leg's.
- Every parameter lives in the config object. Construction code reads
  values, never defines them. A literal in a build step is a value the
  user cannot change without editing your code.
- Name the unit in the variable: `height_m`, `tilt_deg`. Never convert
  implicitly.
- Declare the frame. State where a part's origin sits and which way it
  faces before you place anything relative to it. A transform you
  assumed is a transform that will be wrong once.

## Preconditions are explicit, never inherited

Before an operation runs, know — do not assume — the state it needs:
which object is active, what is selected, which mode you are in. State
that does not survive between chunks: an object created in one chunk is
not still selected in the next.

Link every object into the scene as you create it. An unlinked object
has no depsgraph instance, so every measurement you take of it returns
the stored value instead of the evaluated one, and reads plausible while
being wrong.

## Measure last

Verify after the LAST operation that touches a part, not when you build
it. A boolean, a join or a modifier apply moves what you already
checked, and a measurement taken before them certifies nothing.
